[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate silent 20s]

[candidate · 0:20] Let me lay out how I'd think about this.

Profit is revenue minus costs, and since revenue is actually up 15%, the margin collapse from 24% to 6% — 18 points — has to be driven mostly by the cost side, or by a shift in revenue mix toward lower-margin items.

So I'd break it into three buckets. First, revenue: is the 15% growth coming from new stores, more transactions per store, or price increases? Mix matters a lot — if they've shifted toward lower-margin food or added locations with weaker economics, that drags margin even with growth.

Second, costs: split fixed versus variable. On the variable side, coffee beans, milk, cups, and store labor. On the fixed side, rent, corporate overhead, marketing, and any expansion costs from opening new stores.

Third, external factors: commodity prices, wage inflation, competitive pricing pressure.

To start, could I get the cost breakdown as a percent of revenue, two years ago versus today?
