[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 1:39] Understood. The 24% on-price derivation is clear. You flagged volume response as the risk with a price move that large. Beyond a straight price increase, what else could Brew & Bean do to recover the margin?

[candidate · 1:46] A few levers, and honestly some of these are more attractive than a 24% price hike.

Procurement first. Three-quarters of COGS is dairy, packaging, and food, and that bundle is up 37.5%. That's where I'd push: renegotiate supplier contracts, consolidate vendors across all 200 stores for volume leverage, lock in forward contracts or hedges on the volatile inputs instead of buying spot. A chain this size has buying power it may not be using.

Second, product mix. Without category-level gross margins I'm inferring, but if food and cold drinks are the inflating, lower-margin end, steer customers toward high-margin espresso drinks through menu design and placement.

Third, portion and waste control — spoilage, cup sizes, free-pour milk.

Fourth, selective pricing rather than blanket: raise on price-insensitive items, hold on traffic drivers.

Fifth, overhead — it's drifted two points with a flat footprint, so there's scrutiny available there.

Realistically I'd blend: maybe 10-12% price plus procurement and mix to close the rest of the 38.
